{"id":"neet-2024-q174","pn":174,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Locomotion and Movement","st":"Types of joints","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Fibrous joints — I. Adjacent vertebrae, limited movement\nB. Cartilaginous joints — II. Humerus and Pectoral girdle, rotational movement\nC. Hinge joints — III. Skull, don't allow any movement\nD. Ball and socket joints — IV. Knee, help in locomotion","o":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Fibrous joints allow no movement; flat skull bones join end to end by dense fibrous tissue as sutures.\nCartilaginous joints lie between adjacent vertebrae and allow limited movement.\nHinge joints are synovial joints in the knee that help locomotion.\nBall and socket joints are synovial joints between humerus and pectoral girdle and allow rotation.\nSo A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.\nNote: The printed answer is [2], but the printed solution names option (4); the working agrees with option (2)."}
